<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Carpi</h1><p class="st-meta">1319 CE – 1527 CE · capital Carpi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Pio family's small Emilian lordship, whose humanist prince Alberto III Pio made Carpi a Renaissance showpiece.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1319 the Pio family, local nobles, took control of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carpi">Carpi</a>, a town between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mantua">Mantua</a>, with the support of the Bonacolsi. They ruled it as imperial vicars, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">Este</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">Gonzaga</a> as powerful neighbours.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Pio governed a small farming territory and its walled town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberto-iii-pio">Alberto III Pio</a>, pupil and patron of the printer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius">Aldus Manutius</a>, rebuilt <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carpi">Carpi</a> around a huge new square with the castle, a new cathedral and porticoes. A diplomat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, he sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Italian Wars</a> and quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a>. The family often divided the lordship among relatives.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Alberto's support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> cost him his state: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V's</a> forces occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carpi">Carpi</a>, and in 1527 the emperor gave it to Alfonso I d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">Este</a>. Carpi then became part of the Este lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-modena-and-reggio">Duchy of Modena and Reggio</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1319 CE – 1527 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.95%;width:4.14%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1319 CE – 1527 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Carpi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manfredo Pio, Alberto III Pio</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Emilian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Carpi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1423&amp;focus=lordship-of-carpi" aria-label="Lordship of Carpi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4951 847 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Carpi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5271 1103L5229 694M5271 1103L5261 855"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5261 855h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5271 1103h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5271 1103h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Carpi. <a href="/atlas?year=1423&amp;focus=lordship-of-carpi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
